WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.272
A capstone should begin with a question that a metric and data protocol can actually answer.

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:12.345
“Works on new videos” is different from “works on more frames from known videos”, so the

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:16.140
intended generalization determines how samples must be grouped before splitting.

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:22.411
Frames in a video are related. A frame-level random split can put close relatives into both

00:00:22.411 --> 00:00:23.847
training and test sets.

00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:29.903
Whole-video splitting keeps each video in one role. The number of images is larger than the

00:00:29.903 --> 00:00:31.287
number of independent groups.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:38.040
How many independent groups does a hundred-frame dataset really contain in this example? Pause here and

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.990
make a prediction before continuing.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:48.078
For this comparison only, assume each frame is assigned independently to test with probability q. A

00:00:48.078 --> 00:00:55.056
video is mixed unless all five frames go to one side. For our starting inputs, Train

00:00:55.056 --> 00:01:00.290
videos: 16. Test videos: 4. Expected mixed videos under frame split: 13.44.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.627
This is an expectation under independent frame assignments, not a deterministic count from a fixed-size split.

00:01:07.627 --> 00:01:11.040
Group splitting has zero mixed videos by construction.

00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:17.140
Write the claim and protocol before selecting an architecture. The split is part of what your

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:17.837
result means.

